1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,760
Hi, in this video we're going to look at identifying supported HTTP methods with BurpSuite.

2
00:00:11,760 --> 00:00:16,400
Due to misconfiguration, some websites support additional HTTP methods that can be useful

3
00:00:16,400 --> 00:00:19,000
to an attacker in a number of ways.

4
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:24,660
They might enable an attacker to perform destructive actions like modifying sensitive data or uploading

5
00:00:24,660 --> 00:00:27,040
malicious files.

6
00:00:27,040 --> 00:00:32,200
Maybe bypass flawed access controls and other weak defences, or disclose additional information

7
00:00:32,200 --> 00:00:36,400
about the website and its infrastructure.

8
00:00:36,400 --> 00:00:40,960
Some websites may ignore the method completely, and respond in the same way even if you specify

9
00:00:40,960 --> 00:00:43,940
an arbitrary or non-standard method.

10
00:00:43,940 --> 00:00:48,780
This might enable you to bypass WAFs and weak input filters, as well as defences against

11
00:00:48,780 --> 00:00:51,540
cross-site attacks.

12
00:00:51,540 --> 00:00:56,420
Sending an options request sometimes causes the server to tell you which methods it supports,

13
00:00:56,420 --> 00:01:00,360
but the information can be misleading or incomplete.

14
00:01:00,360 --> 00:01:06,140
The most reliable approach for identifying supported HTTP methods is to send a request using each

15
00:01:06,140 --> 00:01:10,100
potential method and check the responses.

16
00:01:10,100 --> 00:01:15,100
This can be time consuming, but you can automate this using BurpIntruder.

17
00:01:15,100 --> 00:01:21,540
Once you have mapped your target website, the simplest approach is to test a single endpoint.

18
00:01:21,540 --> 00:01:25,760
Supported methods are often fairly consistent across the website, as they are usually the

19
00:01:25,760 --> 00:01:31,220
result of server-level configuration and the default configuration of backend frameworks.

20
00:01:31,220 --> 00:01:34,360
There can always be exceptions, though.

21
00:01:34,360 --> 00:01:39,800
To enumerate supported methods on a single endpoint, first identify a relevant request, such as this

22
00:01:39,800 --> 00:01:45,020
request for the home page of one of the Web Security Academy labs.

23
00:01:45,020 --> 00:01:53,600
Highlight the request method, right-click, and send the request to Intruder.

24
00:01:53,600 --> 00:01:57,920
In BurpIntruder on the Positions tab, because we highlighted the request method before sending

25
00:01:57,920 --> 00:02:04,480
it to Intruder, the method should have been highlighted and automatically marked as a payload position.

26
00:02:04,480 --> 00:02:07,820
We'll keep the attack type set to Sniper.

27
00:02:07,820 --> 00:02:16,800
Then when we go to the Payloads tab, under Payload Sets, select Simple List.

28
00:02:16,800 --> 00:02:22,520
Under the Payload Settings, add a list of HTTP methods you want to test.

29
00:02:22,520 --> 00:02:27,940
If you're using BurpSuite Professional, you can use the Add From List dropdown and choose

30
00:02:27,940 --> 00:02:32,580
the built-in HTTP verbs word list.

31
00:02:32,580 --> 00:02:37,640
In BurpSuite Community, you'll need to add your list manually and make sure you include

32
00:02:37,640 --> 00:02:44,920
an arbitrary, non-existent method to see how the server responds.

33
00:02:44,920 --> 00:02:49,240
Click Start Attack.

34
00:02:49,240 --> 00:02:54,240
When the attack is finished, review the responses for any interesting behaviour.

35
00:02:54,240 --> 00:02:58,340
Make sure you take a close look and don't just rely on the HTTP status code.

36
00:02:58,340 --> 00:03:05,540
This can be misleading.

37
00:03:05,540 --> 00:03:11,160
You may also want to enumerate methods for multiple endpoints, as you may encounter inconsistent

38
00:03:11,160 --> 00:03:17,000
behaviour in responses to the same methods on different parts of the site.

39
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:21,920
This can occur when web sites are maintained by multiple teams of developers who may configure

40
00:03:21,920 --> 00:03:24,520
their own custom request handling.

41
00:03:24,520 --> 00:03:28,540
You might also find that specific endpoints behave differently.

42
00:03:28,540 --> 00:03:33,580
To do this, go to Target, Sitemap.

43
00:03:33,580 --> 00:03:40,600
Right click on a host and choose Copy URLs in this host.

44
00:03:40,600 --> 00:03:46,960
Send a GET request for the target host to BurpIntruder.

45
00:03:46,960 --> 00:03:56,060
In BurpIntruder, go to the Positions tab and choose Attack Type Cluster Bomb.

46
00:03:56,060 --> 00:04:03,220
We can then select the request method and click Add to create the first payload position.

47
00:04:03,220 --> 00:04:13,820
And then select the request path and click Add again to create a second payload position.

48
00:04:13,820 --> 00:04:24,220
On the Payloads tab, for the first position, select Simple List and add a HTTP verbs word list.

49
00:04:24,220 --> 00:04:29,000
If we're using BurpSuite Professional, we can choose Add from List and select the built-in

50
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:34,140
HTTP verbs word list.

51
00:04:34,140 --> 00:04:39,100
Now set the details for the second payload position by going to the Payload Sets and switching to

52
00:04:39,100 --> 00:04:42,620
Payload 2.

53
00:04:42,620 --> 00:04:58,440
Under Payload Settings, click Paste to add the list of URLs you copied from the sitemap.

54
00:04:58,440 --> 00:05:01,580
Under Payload Processing Rules, click Add.

55
00:05:01,580 --> 00:05:05,740
Create a match and replace rule that replaces the scheme and hostname of your URLs with an

56
00:05:05,740 --> 00:05:12,740
empty string.

57
00:05:12,740 --> 00:05:18,020
Then, under Payload Encoding, deselect the URL Encode These Characters checkbox to prevent

58
00:05:18,020 --> 00:05:22,520
the forward slash characters from being encoded.

59
00:05:22,520 --> 00:05:24,740
Click Start Attack.

60
00:05:24,740 --> 00:05:31,740
Again, once the attack is completed, you can review the responses for interesting behaviour.

61
00:05:31,740 --> 00:05:35,740
Remember, don't just rely on the HTTP status code.

62
00:05:35,740 --> 00:05:36,740
It can be misleading.

